.TH GGE\-GRID 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-grid \- scrolling, selectable grids for inventories, rosters and level selects
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \fBGrid\fR (grid.h) lays out N equal cells, cols per row, inside a window of a fixed number of rows, and keeps a cursor over one of them. Rows that do not fit scroll, and the window always follows the cursor. It handles keyboard (arrows or WASD, Enter or space), gamepad (dpad, PAD_SOUTH) and mouse (hover selects, click activates, wheel scrolls). Unlike a \fBMenu\fR it has no labels and no actions: the grid knows where cell i is on screen and which one is selected, and the game draws the contents and acts on the selection. It is presentation state: one static per scene, set up in on_enter.
.PP
The grid is a view over a list the game already keeps (inventory slots, a packed array of roster indices, level ids). Item i in the grid is item i in that list; the grid never stores the items themselves.
.PP
.SH A ROSTER SCREEN
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include "grid.h"
static Grid    grid;
static int16_t housed[CAT_ROSTER_SIZE];      /* roster indices of every cat at home */
static int     housed_n;
\&
static void rebuild(const GameState *g) {
  housed_n = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < CAT_ROSTER_SIZE; i++)
    if (g\->cat_roster[i].tamed) housed[housed_n++] = (int16_t)i;
  grid_set_count(&grid, housed_n);           /* clamps the cursor if the list shrank */
}
\&
static void on_enter(Engine *e, Scene *s) {
  (void)s;
  grid_init(&grid, 40, 80, 80, 80, 6, 4);    /* 6 across, 4 rows visible, 80 px square tiles */
  rebuild(e\->user);
}
static void handle_input(Engine *e, Scene *s, const Input *in) {
  (void)s;
  GameState *g = e\->user;
  if (grid_handle_input(&grid, in) && grid.selected >= 0) {
    toggle_party(g, housed[grid.selected]);  /* the game's action on the chosen item */
    rebuild(g);
  }
  if (key_pressed(in, KEY_ESCAPE)) engine_pop(e);
}
static void render(Engine *e, Scene *s, float alpha) {
  (void)s; (void)alpha;
  GameState *g = e\->user;
  draw_rect(rect(0, 0, e\->logical_w, e\->logical_h), rgba(0, 0, 0, 170));
  grid_render(&grid);
  for (int i = grid_first_visible(&grid); i < grid_end_visible(&grid); i++) {
    Rect c = grid_cell_rect(&grid, i);
    const Cat *cat = &g\->cat_roster[housed[i]];
    sprite_draw(&cats, cat\->look, c.x + 8, c.y + 8, 0, FLIP_NONE);
    draw_debug_text(c.x + 4, c.y + c.h \- 10, COLOR_WHITE, cat\->name);
  }
  grid_render_scrollbar(&grid, 6);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
grid_handle_input returns true on the frame the selection is activated; the scene reads \fBgrid.selected\fR and does whatever that means for its list. After anything that changes the list, call grid_set_count (or a rebuild that does) so the cursor cannot point past the end.
.PP
.SH THE CALLS
.TP
\fBgrid_init(g, x, y, cell_w, cell_h, cols, rows)\fR
reset: window position, horizontal and vertical pitch, columns, visible rows; no items, cursor \-1.
.TP
\fBgrid_set_count(g, n)\fR
how many items; clamps cursor and scroll. Call whenever the list changes length.
.TP
\fBgrid_handle_input(g, in)\fR
navigation, wheel, hover; returns true when the selection is activated. From handle_input.
.TP
\fBgrid_render(g)\fR, \fBgrid_render_scrollbar(g, w)\fR
cell backgrounds and the selection outline; a scrollbar when rows overflow. From render, before your cell contents.
.TP
\fBgrid_cell_rect(g, i)\fR, \fBgrid_first_visible(g)\fR, \fBgrid_end_visible(g)\fR
where to draw item i, and the range of items currently in the window.
.TP
\fBgrid_select(g, i)\fR, \fBgrid_move(g, dx, dy)\fR, \fBgrid_scroll_by(g, rows)\fR
move the cursor or the window from code: preselect, restore after a rebuild, page with PAGEUP/PAGEDOWN.
.TP
\fBgrid_hit(g, mx, my)\fR, \fBgrid_window(g)\fR, \fBgrid_visible(g, i)\fR
queries for custom mouse handling or drawing.
Fields you may set after grid_init: \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR, \fBgap\fR, \fBwrap\fR, \fBcolor\fR and \fBcolor_selected\fR.
.PP
.SH TILES OR ROWS
The two pitches are independent. Equal values give square tiles (an inventory, a sprite roster). A single column with a wide cell_w and a short cell_h gives a list: one item per row with room for a name, a level and a status side by side, scrolling vertically like any other grid. The cursor, wheel and hover work the same either way.
.PP
.SH FIXED GRIDS
An inventory with a constant number of slots is a grid whose count never changes and whose rows equal its total rows, so nothing scrolls. Set \fBwrap\fR to true so the cursor wraps around the edges the way a small fixed grid is expected to; leave it false for anything that scrolls, where running off the bottom should stop rather than jump to the top.
.PP
.SH CLIPPING
Only whole rows are ever shown: the drawing loop covers grid_first_visible..grid_end_visible and nothing else, so no clip rectangle is needed and cells never appear half cut off. A pixel\-smooth scroll (rows sliding by fractions of a cell) is not provided.
.PP
.SH MOUSE BEHAVIOR
While the pointer rests over a cell that cell stays selected, overriding keyboard movement until the mouse leaves the window, as with a Menu. The wheel scrolls the window without moving the cursor, so the cursor may leave the window; the next arrow press brings the window back to it.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Setting \fBcount\fR directly: the cursor and scroll are not clamped. Use grid_set_count.
.IP \(bu 2
Drawing every item instead of the visible range: items scrolled out of the window get drawn above or below it.
.IP \(bu 2
Calling grid_init in render: the grid resets every frame.
.IP \(bu 2
Acting on \fBselected\fR without checking it is not \-1: an empty grid has no selection.
.IP \(bu 2
Using WASD for both the grid and something else in the same scene: the grid consumes nothing, so both will react.
